// IELTS Speaking Part 2: 50 original cue cards (person, place, object, event, experience, activity, media) with
// 1-minute prep and 2-minute speaking timers, a self-check list for each, and natural model answers for most.
// Everything is original; British spelling throughout.
// Card: [sub, level, 'Describe ...', [three bullets], 'explain ...', notes, model paragraphs or null]
const C = [];
const add = (sub, level, title, bullets, explain, notes, model) => C.push({ sub, level, title, bullets, explain, notes, model });

// ---------- person (8) ----------
add('person', 2, 'Describe a teacher who helped you to learn something', ['who the teacher was', 'what subject they taught you', 'how they helped you'], 'why this teacher was important to you',
  'Mr Rao, maths, age 14, patient, extra help, confidence',
  [`I'd like to talk about Mr Rao, who taught me mathematics when I was fourteen. He was a tall, quiet man who always wore the same grey jacket, and he had a very calm way of speaking.`,
   `Maths was my weakest subject at that time. I used to panic when I saw a page of equations, and I was close to giving up completely. Mr Rao noticed this, and one afternoon he asked me to stay behind after class. Instead of telling me off for my low marks, he asked me to show him exactly where I got stuck.`,
   `That was how he helped me. He never gave me the answer straight away. He would draw a small diagram on the board and say, "Try the first step only." Then he waited, sometimes for a full minute, until I found the next step myself. He also gave me extra practice sheets and checked them every Friday, which made me feel that someone really cared.`,
   `Within a few months, my marks went from forty per cent to nearly eighty. More importantly, I stopped being afraid of numbers.`,
   `The reason why he was so important to me is that he changed the way I see difficulty. Before, I thought that being bad at something meant it was impossible. Now I know that it just means I haven't found the right method yet. That idea has helped me in my career and in learning languages, so I'm still grateful to him today.`]);
add('person', 2, 'Describe a person in your family whom you admire', ['who this person is', 'what they do', 'what they are like'], 'why you admire them',
  'my grandmother, village, seamstress, hardworking, generous, lessons',
  [`The person I'd like to describe is my grandmother on my mother's side. She's seventy-six now and still lives in the small village where she was born.`,
   `She worked as a seamstress for most of her life. When I was little, her front room was always full of colourful fabric, and I remember the sound of her sewing machine from early in the morning. She made clothes for half the village, including the school uniforms for dozens of children, and even now she repairs things for neighbours as a favour.`,
   `As for her character, she's strict but very warm. She expects everyone to be polite and to finish their food, but she's also the first person to laugh at a joke. She is incredibly generous, too. Whenever somebody in the village is ill, she turns up with a pot of soup, without being asked.`,
   `I admire her mainly because of how she brought up her four children with very little money. She never complained. She worked long days, saved a little each month and made sure that all of them went to school. Two of them became teachers, which makes her extremely proud.`,
   `On top of that, she taught me that being kind doesn't cost anything. Whenever I feel stressed about work, I think about her and remember how much she achieved with so few resources. That puts my own problems into perspective.`]);
add('person', 3, 'Describe a friend who is good at making people feel comfortable', ['who the friend is', 'how and when you met', 'what they do to make others feel at ease'], 'why this is a valuable quality',
  'Tomás, met at university, listens, jokes, remembers details, valuable',
  [`I'd like to describe my friend Tomás, whom I've known since the first week of university. We were both new to the city, and he was the one who said hello to me in a crowded lecture hall.`,
   `He's a tall, relaxed person with a big smile, and he studies architecture. We met when I was sitting alone at the back, looking rather lost, and he simply sat next to me and asked whether I'd understood the timetable. That small gesture started our friendship, and it has lasted for six years.`,
   `He makes people comfortable in several ways. First, he's an excellent listener. When you talk, he looks at you, nods and asks follow-up questions, so you feel that your story matters. Second, he has a gentle sense of humour. If someone is nervous, he'll make a joke about himself rather than about them, which breaks the tension immediately. Third, he remembers details. He'll ask how your sister's exam went or whether your cold is better, and people love that.`,
   `I remember a party at my flat where a shy classmate was standing alone by the door. Tomás went over, handed her a drink and introduced her to two people who shared her interest in photography. Within ten minutes, she was laughing in the middle of a group.`,
   `I think this is a valuable quality because many people feel awkward in new situations, whether it's a new school, a new job or a new city. Someone who can make them feel welcome can change the whole experience. It also builds trust, so people share ideas more freely.`,
   `I've tried to copy his habits, especially listening carefully, and I've noticed that conversations are now much easier for me.`]);
add('person', 3, 'Describe a famous person you would like to meet', ['who the person is', 'what they are famous for', 'what you would ask or say to them'], 'why you would like to meet this person', 'scientist, Marie Curie type, curiosity, questions, inspiration', null);
add('person', 2, 'Describe a neighbour you get on well with', ['who the neighbour is', 'how long you have known them', 'what you do together or for each other'], 'why you get on well', 'Mrs Alvi, next door, tea, shopping, trust, kindness',
  [`I'd like to tell you about Mrs Alvi, who lives in the flat next door. She's in her sixties, a retired nurse, and she has lived in our building for over twenty years, which is much longer than anyone else.`,
   `We've been neighbours for about six years, since my family moved in. At the beginning, we just nodded in the corridor, but one winter our heating broke down and she invited us into her flat for hot tea. After that, we started chatting regularly.`,
   `These days we do small things for each other. I carry her heavy shopping up the stairs on Saturdays, and I helped her to set up a video call to talk to her grandchildren abroad. In return, she waters our plants when we're away, and she often leaves a plate of homemade biscuits at our door. Once a month, she also comes for dinner and tells us stories about her years in the hospital, which are fascinating.`,
   `I think we get on well because we respect each other's space. She never interferes, and she's never noisy, so there's no tension. We also have a similar sense of humour, and we both like to keep the building clean and friendly.`,
   `Having a neighbour like her makes our home feel safe. If there were an emergency, I know she would help without hesitation, and I'd do the same for her. That sense of community is rare in a big city, so I really value it.`]);
add('person', 3, 'Describe a person who gave you good advice', ['who the person is', 'what the advice was', 'when they gave it to you'], 'why the advice was useful',
  'my manager, first job, ask questions, fear, advice changed habits',
  [`One person who gave me really good advice was my first manager, a woman called Ms Dhillon. I'd just left university and started a job at a small marketing company, and I was extremely nervous.`,
   `She was in her forties, very organised, and she had a reputation for being direct but fair. In my second week, I made a serious mistake: I sent a client the wrong price list. I was sure I'd be fired, but she called me into her office and, instead of shouting, she said something that I still remember. She told me, "Never hide a problem, and always ask early."`,
   `At that time, I hadn't been asking questions because I didn't want to look stupid. She explained that in her experience the people who move forward in their careers are those who ask a lot of questions in the first year, because mistakes are cheaper when they're small. She suggested that I note down my doubts each morning and go through them with her for ten minutes at lunchtime.`,
   `I followed her advice, and it turned out to be extremely helpful. My work improved quickly, and I stopped making careless errors. A few months later, she even gave me responsibility for a small client.`,
   `The advice was useful because it applies beyond that job. Even now, whenever I'm unsure about something, I ask immediately rather than worry in silence. It saves time, reduces stress, and, I think, makes people trust you more.`]);
add('person', 3, 'Describe someone who has an interesting job', ['who the person is', 'what job they do', 'what they say about their work'], 'why you think the job is interesting',
  'cousin, marine biologist, coral reefs, diving, fieldwork, passion',
  [`I'd like to talk about my cousin Elena, who works as a marine biologist. She studies coral reefs, and she spends about half of the year at a research station on a small island.`,
   `Her job involves diving every day to count fish and check the health of the coral. She records everything in waterproof notebooks and then takes the data back to the lab for analysis. She also works with local fishermen to explain how to protect the reef, so she does a bit of teaching as well.`,
   `When we talk, she's always full of enthusiasm. She says the best part is the moment when you first go underwater in the morning, and the whole reef is full of colour. But she's also honest about the difficult side. The work is physically tiring, the equipment is heavy, and she misses her family during the long periods away. She often says that you have to love the sea to do this job.`,
   `I think her work is interesting for several reasons. To begin with, it's very different from an office job, because every day brings new surprises. Secondly, it's meaningful, as coral reefs are damaged by warm water and pollution, and her results help to decide how they are protected. Finally, she's constantly learning, because the sea is so complex that nobody understands all of it.`,
   `I'd love to visit her at the research station someday and try diving with her, although I'm slightly nervous about it. At the very least, her stories have made me more conscious of the ocean.`]);
add('person', 4, 'Describe a person from history that you find interesting', ['who the person was', 'when and where they lived', 'what they did that was important'], 'why you find this person interesting',
  'Ibn Battuta, 14th century, traveller, 30 years, Africa Asia, curiosity',
  [`I'd like to talk about Ibn Battuta, a traveller and scholar from Morocco who lived in the fourteenth century. I first heard about him in a history class, and his story has stayed with me ever since.`,
   `He was born in Tangier in 1304, and at the age of twenty-one he set out on a pilgrimage to Mecca. Instead of returning home, he just kept going. Over nearly thirty years, he travelled across North Africa, the Middle East, Central Asia, India, and even as far as China, covering something like seventy thousand miles. That's astonishing in a time without trains, planes or maps as we know them.`,
   `What makes him important is that, when he came home, he dictated an account of his journeys. It describes cities, markets, foods, religious customs and the people he met, and it has become one of the most valuable records of life in the medieval world. Historians still use it today, although they sometimes have to check some of his stories against other sources.`,
   `For me, he's interesting for a few reasons. First of all, he was driven by curiosity rather than by money or power. Secondly, he faced extreme dangers, including robbers, shipwrecks and illness, and he kept going anyway. Lastly, his book shows how connected the medieval world already was, which is something that many people don't expect.`,
   `Whenever I read about him, I'm reminded that exploring other cultures with an open mind is one of the best ways to learn, and it makes me want to travel more myself.`]);

// ---------- place (8) ----------
add('place', 2, 'Describe a place in your town where you like to relax', ['where it is', 'what it looks like', 'what you do there'], 'why it helps you relax',
  'riverside park, bench, trees, reading, walking, quiet',
  [`I'd like to talk about a small park by the river in my town, which is where I go whenever I need to relax.`,
   `It's about fifteen minutes' walk from my flat, close to the old stone bridge. The park isn't very big, but it's very pretty. There's a long path beside the water, a row of tall willow trees, and a few wooden benches. In spring, there are flowers everywhere, and in autumn the ground is covered with golden leaves.`,
   `I usually go there on Sunday afternoons. I take a book or a flask of tea, find my favourite bench under the biggest tree and just sit for an hour or so. Sometimes I walk along the river as far as the next bridge, and I watch the ducks and the occasional boat. When I'm in a good mood, I take photographs of the light on the water.`,
   `The place helps me relax for several reasons. First, it's quiet. Although it's in the middle of town, the trees block most of the traffic noise, so all I hear is the water and the birds. Second, being near nature makes me feel calmer, and my worries seem smaller. And third, nobody is in a rush there. People walk slowly, so I find that I slow down as well.`,
   `After a stressful week, even half an hour in that park makes me feel like I've had a little holiday. I honestly don't know what I'd do without it.`]);
add('place', 3, 'Describe a place you visited on holiday and would like to visit again', ['where it is', 'when you went and who with', 'what you did there'], 'why you would like to go back',
  'Lisbon, hills, trams, family, food, sunsets, go back',
  [`The place I'd love to return to is Lisbon, the capital of Portugal. I went there two summers ago with my sister and two cousins, and we stayed for a week.`,
   `Lisbon is built on hills, so there are steep streets, colourful tiled houses and old yellow trams that rattle through the narrow lanes. We rented a small flat in an old neighbourhood, with a balcony that looked over the rooftops towards the river.`,
   `Every day, we did something different. We rode the famous tram to the castle, wandered through the old streets, and took a boat trip on the river. One day, we took a short train to a seaside town and swam in the Atlantic, which was much colder than I'd expected. In the evenings, we sat at outdoor restaurants and ate grilled fish and custard tarts, which I fell in love with.`,
   `There are a few reasons why I'd like to go back. The first is the food, which was simple but delicious and surprisingly cheap. The second is the atmosphere. People were relaxed and friendly, and I felt welcome. Another is that we didn't have enough time. We missed several places, such as the palaces in the hills nearby, and I'd love to see them.`,
   `Perhaps the main reason, though, is the memory of sitting on that balcony at sunset with my family, with the whole city turning orange. I'd like to share that feeling with other people, so I'm already saving for another trip.`]);
add('place', 3, 'Describe a historical place you have visited', ['where it is', 'what you saw there', 'what you learned about its history'], 'how you felt about the place',
  'old fort, hilltop, walls, guide, built 400 years ago, awe',
  null);
add('place', 2, 'Describe a restaurant or cafe you like to go to', ['where it is', 'what it is like inside', 'what you usually eat or drink there'], 'why you like it',
  'corner cafe, books on shelves, owner, flat white, cake, friendly',
  [`I'd like to describe a small cafe called The Reading Room, which is on a corner near my office. I discovered it about three years ago when it rained heavily and I ran in to get out of the weather.`,
   `It's a cosy place with only about ten tables. The walls are covered with shelves full of second-hand books, and customers are allowed to borrow them and bring them back later. The furniture is wooden and slightly old-fashioned, and there are always fresh flowers on the counter. In the afternoon, the sun comes through the big window and makes everything look warm and golden.`,
   `I usually go on Saturday mornings, when I'm not working. I order a flat white and a slice of lemon cake, which is baked by the owner herself, and I stay for a couple of hours with a book or my laptop. In winter I sometimes have their vegetable soup with bread, which is excellent.`,
   `There are several reasons why I like it. Most importantly, the atmosphere is relaxed. Nobody hurries you to leave, and there's soft music, so it's perfect for reading or thinking. The staff are friendly, too. They remember my order and often ask how my week was. And the prices are reasonable, which matters to me.`,
   `In a way, it feels like a second living room. When I'm there, I feel I'm part of a small community, which is something I'd miss if the cafe ever closed.`]);
add('place', 3, 'Describe a natural place you have visited, such as a forest, lake or mountain', ['where it is', 'how you got there', 'what you saw and did there'], 'how it made you feel',
  'mountain lake, bus, hike, clear water, picnic, peaceful',
  [`I'd like to talk about a mountain lake that I visited last summer with some friends from work. It's in a national park about three hours north of my city, and it's called Blue Lake, although I think it's more green than blue.`,
   `We got there by hiring a minibus, and then we had to walk for about two hours along a forest path to reach the lake. The trail was quite steep in places, and we were all out of breath, but the air smelt of pine trees and there were small streams to cross, so it wasn't boring at all.`,
   `When we finally arrived, I was speechless. The water was so clear that I could see the stones at the bottom, and the surrounding mountains were reflected on the surface like a mirror. We had a picnic on the shore, and a couple of the more adventurous friends went for a swim, although the water was freezing. I just paddled my feet and took lots of photographs. In the afternoon, we watched a deer come down to drink, which was a magical moment.`,
   `The place made me feel peaceful and also very small, in a good way. In my everyday life, I'm usually surrounded by screens and deadlines, but there I only had to think about where to put my feet next. I felt my mind going quiet.`,
   `Since then, I try to go to the countryside at least once a month. It doesn't have to be as dramatic as that lake, but being in nature always puts me in a better mood and gives me new energy for work.`]);
add('place', 3, 'Describe a building you find beautiful', ['where it is', 'what it looks like', 'what it is used for'], 'why you think it is beautiful', 'central station, glass roof, iron, arches, light, trains, history', null);
add('place', 2, 'Describe a place where you often meet friends', ['where it is', 'what it is like', 'what you do there together'], 'why you choose this place',
  'shopping centre food court, central, cheap, board games, noise, convenient',
  [`The place where I most often meet my friends is a bowling alley in the east of my city. It's part of a large entertainment complex, with a cinema, a few restaurants and an arcade next door.`,
   `Inside, it's loud, bright and a bit chaotic. There are about twenty lanes, with neon lights above them and music playing, and at the end there's a bar where you can order burgers and drinks. It isn't fancy, but it's clean and comfortable, and there are tables where you can sit and chat between turns.`,
   `We usually go on Friday evenings, once or twice a month. There are six or seven of us, and we split into two teams and play a few games. We're all quite bad at bowling, so there's lots of laughter whenever someone throws the ball into the gutter. After that, we order food and talk about our week until the staff start to close up.`,
   `We choose this place for a few practical reasons. It's in the centre of the area where we all live, so nobody has to travel far. It's also quite cheap if you go in a group, as there's a student discount. Most importantly, it gives us an activity to do together, which means conversation never feels forced. If somebody is tired or has had a bad week, they can just play and relax without having to say much.`,
   `I'd say it's become part of our friendship. Even when we're really busy, that Friday evening reminds us to make time for each other.`]);
add('place', 4, 'Describe a place in a city that you think should be protected or preserved', ['where it is', 'what it looks like', 'what happens there'], 'why it should be protected',
  'old market, wooden stalls, 200 years, traders, tradition, threatened by redevelopment',
  null);

// ---------- object (7) ----------
add('object', 2, 'Describe an object that you use every day', ['what it is', 'how long you have had it', 'what you use it for'], 'why it is important to you',
  'water bottle, steel, 3 years, hydration, saves money, habit',
  [`The object I'd like to talk about is my steel water bottle. It's an ordinary thing, but I use it every single day, so it's earned a place in my life.`,
   `It's a dark green bottle, about the size of a large mug, with a screw-on lid and a few dents on the bottom from falling off tables. My brother gave it to me for my birthday three years ago, and I've carried it nearly everywhere since then, to the office, to the gym and on holiday.`,
   `I mainly use it to keep myself hydrated. I fill it in the morning before work and again at lunchtime, and I try to finish at least two bottles a day. In winter, I fill it with hot water and a slice of ginger, because it keeps drinks warm for hours. In summer, it keeps water cold even in the heat, which is quite impressive.`,
   `It matters to me for a few reasons. For one thing, it has changed my habits. Before I owned it, I hardly drank any water and I often had headaches, whereas now I feel much more alert. For another, it saves money and plastic, because I don't have to buy bottled water anymore. I worked out that I've avoided buying around seven hundred plastic bottles, which is a nice thought.`,
   `And there's a sentimental side, too. Every time I pick it up, I remember my brother's gift. I'd be genuinely upset if I lost it, even though a new one would cost only a few pounds.`]);
add('object', 3, 'Describe a gift you gave someone that you were pleased with', ['what the gift was', 'who you gave it to', 'how you chose it'], 'why you were pleased with it',
  'framed photo, my mother, 50th birthday, old trip, printed, tears',
  [`I'd like to describe a gift that I gave my mother for her fiftieth birthday, which was a framed collage of photographs from our family's life.`,
   `It was quite a big frame, made of light wood, and it held about thirty pictures arranged in the shape of a heart. They went back to my parents' wedding, with a very serious black-and-white photo of my grandparents, and then moved through holidays, school plays and festivals until last year. I also added a small handwritten note at the bottom.`,
   `I chose it after thinking about what she'd really like. My mother has everything she needs, and she never likes expensive things, so I knew that I had to find something meaningful. I spent about two weekends going through old albums at my grandparents' house and scanning the pictures. I asked my sister to help with the layout, and we kept it a secret by working at her flat.`,
   `I was pleased with it for several reasons. Firstly, my mother's reaction was wonderful. When she unwrapped it, she went quiet, then started laughing at an old photo of my father with a terrible haircut, and finally she had tears in her eyes. Secondly, I put real effort and time into it, which made it feel personal rather than just something I'd bought.`,
   `It now hangs in our hallway, and every visitor stops to look. I think it showed me that the best presents don't need to cost much. They just need to show that you've been paying attention.`]);
add('object', 3, 'Describe something you own that is very old', ['what it is', 'how you got it', 'how old it is'], 'why you keep it', 'grandfather watch, inherited, 70 years, wind it, family story', null);
add('object', 2, 'Describe a piece of technology that you find useful', ['what it is', 'how often you use it', 'what you use it for'], 'why you find it useful',
  'laptop, work and study, daily, online classes, writing, backup',
  [`I'd like to talk about my laptop, which is probably the most useful piece of technology I own. I bought it about two years ago, when my old computer finally stopped working.`,
   `It's a slim silver model with a fifteen-inch screen, and it's light enough to carry in my backpack. I use it every day, on average for about six hours, mostly for work and study.`,
   `As for what I use it for, there are plenty of things. In the mornings I answer emails and prepare documents. In the evenings I often attend online classes, where I practise English with a teacher in another country. I also use it to write, to edit photographs and, of course, to watch films when I want to relax. At weekends, I video call my family, who live in another city, so it helps me to stay in touch.`,
   `It's useful for several reasons. First, it saves me a huge amount of time. Tasks that took hours in the past, such as sending letters or looking something up in a library, now take a few minutes. Second, it gives me flexibility, because I can work from anywhere, even from a cafe or a train. And third, it lets me learn almost anything, as there's so much free material on the internet.`,
   `I do realise that I depend on it too much. If it broke tomorrow, I'd have a real problem, so I back up my files every week. But, overall, I think it's a tool that has made my life more interesting and efficient.`]);
add('object', 3, 'Describe a book that you have read more than once', ['what the book is', 'what it is about', 'when you first read it'], 'why you have read it more than once',
  'novel, boy and the sea, adventure, age 12, courage, new details',
  [`The book I'd like to describe is a novel called The Old Man and the Sea, which I've read three times. It's quite a short book, only about a hundred pages, which is partly why it's easy to return to.`,
   `It tells the story of an elderly fisherman who hasn't caught a fish for eighty-four days. One day he sails far out into the ocean and hooks an enormous marlin. For three days, he struggles with it, alone in his small boat, and in the end he succeeds, but sharks attack the fish on the way home. The story is simple, yet it's really about courage, pride and accepting defeat.`,
   `I first read it when I was seventeen, as part of a school assignment. To be honest, I thought it was boring at the time, because I wanted more action. A few years later, when I was going through a hard period at university, a friend recommended it again, and I saw it in a completely different way. I read it for the third time last year on a long train journey.`,
   `The reason why I keep going back to it is that it changes as I change. When I was younger, I saw only a fishing story. Now I notice the details about the old man's loneliness and his determination to keep trying, even when the result is uncertain. The language is also beautiful, with short sentences that carry a lot of feeling.`,
   `I think that's the mark of a good book. You can return to it at different stages of life and find something new each time. I'm sure I'll read it again when I'm older.`]);
add('object', 3, 'Describe a piece of clothing that you like to wear', ['what it is', 'where you got it', 'when you wear it'], 'why you like wearing it', 'blue jacket, market, denim, autumn, comfort, memories', null);
add('object', 4, 'Describe an invention that has changed the world', ['what the invention is', 'when and how it was developed', 'how people use it'], 'why you think it is so important',
  'printing press, Gutenberg, 1450, books cheaper, literacy, spread of ideas',
  [`I'd like to talk about the printing press, which was developed by Johannes Gutenberg in Germany around 1450. People often describe it as one of the most important inventions in human history, and I completely agree.`,
   `Before it existed, books had to be copied by hand, usually by monks, which took months for a single copy. Naturally, books were rare and expensive, so only the rich and the church owned them. Gutenberg combined several existing ideas, including metal letters that could be moved around and reused, an oil-based ink and a press similar to those used for making wine. With this, a workshop could print hundreds of pages a day.`,
   `Over the following decades, people used it for Bibles, textbooks, pamphlets, maps and later newspapers. Today the same basic idea survives in modern printing, and even in how we think about copying and sharing information.`,
   `I think it's so important because it changed who could learn. As books became cheaper, more people learned to read, and ideas could spread across countries within a few years. It helped to speed up the Renaissance, scientific discovery and even political change, as people could now read and debate the same texts.`,
   `It also reminds me of today's internet. Both made information far easier to share, and both brought benefits and problems at the same time. For me, knowing this history makes me value every book on my shelf a little more.`]);

// ---------- event (8) ----------
add('event', 2, 'Describe a wedding you attended', ['whose wedding it was', 'where and when it took place', 'what happened during the day'], 'how you felt about it',
  'cousin Rana, last spring, garden, music, dancing, food, joy',
  [`I'd like to describe the wedding of my cousin Rana, which I attended last spring. It was one of the happiest days of the year for our family.`,
   `It took place on a Saturday in April, in a big garden on the outskirts of the city. The weather was perfect, with a clear sky and just enough breeze to keep us cool. The garden was decorated with white and yellow flowers, and there were long tables under the trees.`,
   `The day began at about eleven with the ceremony, which was quite short and very moving. Rana looked beautiful in a cream dress, and her husband was clearly nervous, because he kept dropping the paper with his vows. Afterwards, there was a big lunch with about two hundred guests, and the food was fantastic, with lots of rice dishes, grilled meat and desserts. In the afternoon, a band played, and everyone danced, even my elderly grandfather. We finished the evening by lighting small lanterns and sending them into the sky.`,
   `I felt extremely happy, but also a little emotional. I've known Rana since we were babies, and it was strange to see her starting a new chapter in her life. I also enjoyed seeing relatives I only meet once a year, and I realised how much I'd missed them.`,
   `In the end, I went home exhausted from dancing but with a big smile on my face. It reminded me how important it is to celebrate together with the people you love.`]);
add('event', 3, 'Describe a time when you were very busy', ['when it was', 'what you had to do', 'who, if anyone, helped you'], 'how you felt about it',
  'exams and job, last year, deadline, schedule, friend helped, exhausted but proud',
  [`I'd like to talk about a period last year when I was extremely busy. It lasted for about six weeks, and it was probably the most demanding time of my life.`,
   `At that point, I was finishing my final project at university and, at the same time, working part-time at a bookshop. On top of that, I had two exams scheduled in the same week, and my grandmother was ill, so I visited her every other evening. I remember looking at my calendar and realising that there was hardly a free hour in it.`,
   `To cope, I made a detailed timetable. I got up at six every day, studied for two hours before work, and used my lunch breaks to revise. I gave up almost all of my social life, and I cooked large meals on Sundays so that I wouldn't need to think about dinner. My manager at the bookshop was kind enough to change my shifts, and my friend Daniel helped me by checking my project and testing me before the exams.`,
   `Honestly, I felt exhausted and anxious for most of that period. I slept badly, and I sometimes wondered whether I was doing the right thing. But I also felt a growing sense of pride each time I finished a task. When the exams were over and I submitted my project, I felt both empty and incredibly relieved, and I slept for almost twelve hours.`,
   `Looking back, I'm glad that it happened. It taught me how to plan, how to ask for help and how much I can manage when I really have to. I'd rather not repeat it, though.`]);
add('event', 3, 'Describe a festival or celebration in your country', ['what it is called', 'when it takes place', 'what people do'], 'why it is important to people',
  'harvest festival, autumn, lights, family, sweets, tradition',
  [`I'd like to talk about a harvest festival that is celebrated in the countryside where my family comes from. It takes place every year in late October, once the main crops have been gathered.`,
   `The celebration lasts for three days. In the days before it, people clean their houses, paint the doors and prepare special food. On the first evening, every family lights small oil lamps and places them along the windows and the walls, so the whole village glows. On the second day, there's a big market with music, and children wear colourful costumes and walk from house to house, collecting sweets. The last day is for family. We have a huge meal together, with rice, vegetables and sweet pastries, and the elders tell stories about past harvests.`,
   `The festival matters to people for several reasons. The most obvious one is gratitude. For a farming community, a good harvest means that there will be enough food for the year, so people thank the land and each other for their hard work. It's also a time when everyone returns home. Relatives who live in the cities come back for a few days, so the festival keeps families together.`,
   `Personally, I love the sense of belonging that it creates. I've been to it every year since I was little, and the smell of lamp oil and fresh sweets takes me straight back to my childhood.`,
   `Although modern life is changing a lot of our traditions, I hope that it will continue, because it reminds us where our food comes from and why community is so important.`]);
add('event', 3, 'Describe a sports event you watched or took part in', ['what the event was', 'where it took place', 'what happened'], 'how you felt about it',
  'charity 10km run, city park, last May, friends, tired legs, finished, proud',
  [`The event I'd like to describe is a ten-kilometre charity run that I took part in last May. It was organised by a local hospital to raise money for new equipment.`,
   `It took place in the big park in the centre of my city, and about two thousand people entered. I signed up with three friends from work, as a challenge. None of us was a serious runner, so we trained together for two months, starting with short jogs after work and slowly increasing the distance.`,
   `On the morning of the race, the weather was cool and cloudy, which was ideal. We queued at the start line with all kinds of people, from experienced athletes to families pushing buggies, and some who were dressed as animals. For the first few kilometres, I felt good and I kept a steady pace. Around the seventh kilometre, my legs became very heavy, and I nearly stopped. But the crowd along the path was clapping and shouting names, and a small boy handed me a slice of orange, which gave me the push I needed.`,
   `I crossed the finish line in about sixty-eight minutes, which wasn't fast, but I was thrilled. I felt tired, proud and slightly surprised at myself. We also raised nearly four thousand pounds between us, so it felt worthwhile.`,
   `That experience showed me that I'm capable of more than I think. I now run twice a week, and I've already signed up for next year's race. Of course, I'm hoping to beat my time.`]);
add('event', 2, 'Describe a time when you helped someone', ['who you helped', 'what the problem was', 'how you helped'], 'how you felt afterwards',
  'lost tourist, station, map, walked her, thank you, glad',
  [`I'd like to tell you about a time when I helped a tourist who was lost near my local train station. It happened about a year ago, on a rainy Sunday afternoon.`,
   `I was on my way home from the supermarket, and I noticed an elderly woman standing by the entrance, looking at a paper map and clearly worried. When I asked if she needed help, she explained in slow English that she was visiting from abroad and had to find her hotel, but her phone had run out of battery and she couldn't recognise any of the street names.`,
   `I looked at her booking confirmation and realised that the hotel was in a district about twenty minutes away on foot, but she had been walking in the wrong direction. Rather than just pointing, I decided to walk with her, because it was raining and she had a heavy suitcase. I carried it for her, and on the way we chatted about her trip. She told me that this was her first time travelling alone since her husband had passed away.`,
   `When we arrived, she thanked me several times and even tried to give me money, which I politely refused. She kissed me on the cheek, which was very sweet.`,
   `Afterwards, I felt really good. It was a small thing, but it made her day easier, and it reminded me of how stressful it is to be lost in a place where you don't speak the language. I think that's why I now stop whenever I see a visitor who looks confused. I hope that somebody would do the same for me if I were abroad.`]);
add('event', 3, 'Describe an occasion when you were late', ['when it happened', 'where you were going', 'why you were late'], 'what happened as a result', 'job interview, bus broke down, called ahead, apologised, rescheduled, lesson', null);
add('event', 3, 'Describe a meal that you enjoyed with other people', ['what the meal was', 'who you ate with', 'where it took place'], 'why it was enjoyable', 'dinner on the roof, friends, barbecue, summer, music, laughter', null);
add('event', 4, 'Describe a news story that interested you', ['what the story was about', 'where you read or heard it', 'what you thought about it'], 'why it interested you',
  'rescued whales, local news, community effort, hope, environment, shared',
  [`I'd like to talk about a news story I read last year about a group of fishermen who rescued a young whale caught in a net. It was in the online edition of a national newspaper, and a colleague sent me the link at lunchtime.`,
   `The story was about a small fishing village on the coast. One morning, a few boats found a young humpback whale tangled in a heavy fishing net, unable to swim and clearly exhausted. The fishermen could have simply left it, since the net belonged to someone else and the whale was dangerous, but instead they called the coastguard and stayed by it for nearly six hours. Eventually, with help from trained rescuers, they cut it free. The article included a video where the whale swims away and slaps its tail on the water, almost as if it was saying goodbye.`,
   `As I read it, I was moved, but also thoughtful. The fishermen's income depends on the sea, yet they took a risk for an animal that would never repay them. I also thought about the amount of waste that ends up in the oceans, since the net was the reason the whale was in trouble.`,
   `It interested me for a couple of reasons. First, most news is negative, so a hopeful story is quite unusual. Second, it showed how ordinary people can make a difference without any special equipment, just by showing a bit of care.`,
   `I forwarded the link to my family, and we ended up having a long conversation about protecting the oceans. That's what I like about good news stories. They don't just inform you, they start discussion.`]);

// ---------- experience (7) ----------
add('experience', 3, 'Describe a time when you learned a new skill', ['what the skill was', 'how you learned it', 'what difficulties you had'], 'how you feel about it now',
  'swimming at 25, adult class, fear of water, instructor, practice, confident',
  [`I'd like to talk about the time I learned how to swim, which was when I was twenty-five. It might sound late, but I grew up far from the sea and never had the chance as a child.`,
   `I signed up for an adult beginners' class at the local sports centre, which met twice a week in the evenings. There were about eight of us, ranging from a student of nineteen to a man of sixty. Our instructor, a cheerful woman called Priya, started with the basics, like blowing bubbles and floating with a board.`,
   `The biggest difficulty was fear. Whenever my feet couldn't touch the bottom, I panicked and swallowed water. For the first month I hardly made any progress, and I even considered giving up because I felt embarrassed in front of the others. However, Priya was very encouraging, and she suggested that I practise on my own on Sunday mornings, when the pool was quieter. I went regularly, and gradually my fear shrank.`,
   `After about three months, I managed to swim a full length of the pool without stopping. I was so happy that I nearly cried in the water.`,
   `Now I swim twice a week, and I've even tried the sea on holiday. I feel very proud of it, because it was something I never thought I could do. It also taught me that it's never too late to learn, and that the first step is usually the hardest. I'd encourage anyone who is afraid to start in the same way.`]);
add('experience', 3, 'Describe a time when you had to wait for something', ['what you were waiting for', 'how long you waited', 'what you did while you waited'], 'how you felt about it',
  'university results, three weeks, anxious, distracted, walks, relieved',
  [`I'd like to describe the time I waited for my university entrance results, which took almost three weeks. It felt like the longest wait of my life.`,
   `I'd taken the exams at the end of May, and the results were due in the middle of June. At that time, I'd applied to study engineering, and my place depended on getting good marks, so there was a lot of pressure. Every day I checked my phone, even though I knew nothing would arrive before the official date.`,
   `While I was waiting, I tried to keep busy. I helped my father paint our garden fence, I went for long walks with my dog, and I started a small online course in photography, just to take my mind off the exams. In the evenings, I met friends who were in the same situation, and we mostly talked about anything except the results.`,
   `Emotionally, it was a rollercoaster. In the mornings I felt confident, but by night I'd imagine all the questions that I might have answered wrongly. I slept badly, and my mother kept telling me to stop worrying, which of course made no difference.`,
   `When the day finally came, I opened the email with shaking hands and discovered that I'd passed with better marks than I'd expected. I felt an enormous relief, and I think I shouted loud enough for the neighbours to hear.`,
   `Looking back, I learned that worrying doesn't change the outcome. Now, when I'm waiting for something important, I try to stay busy and trust that I did my best.`]);
add('experience', 4, 'Describe an occasion when you disagreed with someone', ['who you disagreed with', 'what the disagreement was about', 'how you dealt with it'], 'what you learned from it',
  'colleague, project plan, deadline vs quality, talked privately, compromise, listen',
  [`I'd like to talk about a disagreement that I had with a colleague, Marcus, about the plan for a project last year. We were both responsible for a report for an important client.`,
   `The problem was that we had very different priorities. Marcus wanted to finish it as soon as possible, because the deadline was close, and he was happy to leave out some of the analysis. I, on the other hand, thought that quality was more important, since the client would read every page. We argued about this in a meeting, and the discussion became rather tense, with both of us raising our voices slightly.`,
   `Afterwards, I realised that this wasn't helping, so I asked him to have a coffee with me the next morning. I told him that I understood his concern about time and asked him to explain exactly which parts worried him. He listened, and then he explained that his previous manager had punished him for missing a deadline, so he was anxious about it. That changed my attitude, because I saw that his position had a real reason behind it.`,
   `In the end, we compromised. We agreed to include the most important analysis in the main report and put the rest in an appendix, which we could finish after the deadline. The client was satisfied, and Marcus and I worked well together after that.`,
   `The main thing I learned was to listen first. When two people disagree, they often argue about the position without understanding the reasons behind it. If you ask questions calmly, it's usually possible to find a solution that works for both.`]);
add('experience', 3, 'Describe a time when you tried food that was new to you', ['what the food was', 'where and with whom you ate it', 'what it was like'], 'whether you would eat it again', 'sushi, Japanese restaurant, colleagues, raw fish, nervous, surprisingly good', null);
add('experience', 3, 'Describe a time when you were in a crowded place', ['where you were', 'why there were so many people', 'what you did'], 'how you felt about it', 'concert, stadium, standing, noise, friend, lost phone, excitement and stress', null);
add('experience', 3, 'Describe a journey that you remember well', ['where you went', 'how you travelled', 'what happened on the way'], 'why you remember it',
  'night train, mountains, window seat, stranger shared food, sunrise, friendship',
  [`The journey I'd like to describe was an overnight train trip I took across the mountains about four years ago. I was travelling alone from my city to a town near the northern border to attend a friend's wedding.`,
   `The train left at nine in the evening, and the journey was supposed to take eleven hours. I'd booked a place in a shared compartment with six beds, which meant that I'd be sleeping in the same small room as strangers. At first I felt a bit uncomfortable, but a middle-aged couple in the next beds quickly started chatting to me and offered me tea from a flask.`,
   `As the evening went on, we shared snacks and stories. They told me about their grandchildren, and I told them about the wedding and my job. At some point in the night, the train stopped for almost an hour because of heavy snow on the line, and everyone got up to look out of the windows. The whole landscape was white and silent, and I remember standing in the corridor in my socks, thinking that it was the most peaceful sight I'd ever seen.`,
   `I finally fell asleep, and I was woken by the sun rising over the mountains. The peaks were pink and gold, and the couple smiled and said, "That's your reward for being patient."`,
   `I remember this trip so well because it was unplanned in the best way. Nothing dramatic happened, but the kindness of strangers and the beauty of the snow made it special. Since then, I've always preferred trains to planes, because the journey itself becomes part of the holiday.`]);
add('experience', 4, 'Describe a time when you had to make an important decision', ['what the decision was', 'what options you had', 'how you made the decision'], 'whether you think it was the right decision',
  'job offer abroad vs stay, family, pros and cons list, mentor, accepted, right choice',
  [`I'd like to talk about the decision I made two years ago, when I was offered a job in another country. It was one of the most difficult choices of my life.`,
   `At that time, I had a stable job in my home city, with colleagues I liked and a flat near my family. Then a company abroad contacted me and offered me a position with a higher salary and more responsibility. The problem was that I'd have to leave my family and friends and start from nothing in a country where I knew nobody.`,
   `I had two options: stay in my comfortable situation or take a risk. To decide, I did several things. First, I made a long list of the pros and cons, which actually made me realise that many of my worries were about the unknown rather than about real problems. Then I talked to my parents and to a former manager, who'd worked abroad himself. He told me that the first six months would be hard but that it would change my career. Finally, I asked myself which choice I'd regret more in ten years, and the answer was clear.`,
   `So I accepted the offer. The first few months were indeed lonely, and I often felt homesick. But slowly I made friends, learned the local language and found new interests.`,
   `I'm now convinced it was the right decision. I've developed professionally, gained confidence and learned a lot about myself. That said, I admit that it isn't the right choice for everyone, and it was only because I had my family's support and a way back that I was able to take the risk.`]);

// ---------- activity (6) ----------
add('activity', 2, 'Describe a hobby you enjoy doing in your free time', ['what the hobby is', 'how you started it', 'how often you do it'], 'why you enjoy it',
  'drawing, sketchbook, age 15, evenings, relaxing, improving',
  [`The hobby I'd like to talk about is drawing. I've been doing it since I was about fifteen, and it's now an important part of my week.`,
   `I started because of a boring summer holiday. I had finished all my books, and my aunt, who is a designer, gave me a sketchbook and a set of pencils and said, "Draw whatever you see." At first, my pictures were terrible, but I kept at it, and after a few months I noticed some improvement.`,
   `These days I draw three or four times a week, usually in the evening after dinner. I like to sketch things around me, such as my desk, the view from my window or people in cafes. On weekends, I sometimes take my sketchbook to the park and draw trees and buildings for an hour or two. I've filled about ten sketchbooks so far, and I keep them on a shelf.`,
   `There are several reasons why I enjoy it. The first is that it relaxes me. When I'm concentrating on lines and shadows, I forget about my emails and deadlines. The second is that it makes me look at the world more carefully, as I notice colours and shapes that I'd normally ignore. And the third is that I can see myself improving. When I look back at my old drawings, I'm both amused and proud.`,
   `It's also a very cheap hobby, because all I need is a pencil and paper. I'd recommend it to anyone who wants a quiet way to switch off.`]);
add('activity', 3, 'Describe an outdoor activity that you enjoy', ['what the activity is', 'where you do it', 'who you do it with'], 'why you enjoy it',
  'hill walking, weekends, club, views, fresh air, fitness, friends',
  [`The outdoor activity I enjoy most is hill walking. I do it about once a month, usually on a Saturday, and I've been doing it for around four years now.`,
   `I normally go in the hills about an hour from my city, where there are marked trails of different lengths. I belong to a small walking group, with about ten people, who are all different ages. We meet at a car park early in the morning, share lifts and then walk for five or six hours, with a stop at the top for a picnic. We bring thermos flasks, sandwiches and, most importantly, plenty of chocolate.`,
   `I started because a colleague invited me along when I was feeling tired and stressed. I wasn't convinced that it would be fun, as I thought walking for hours would be boring. However, the first time I reached a summit and saw the whole valley below me, I was hooked.`,
   `I enjoy it for several reasons. For a start, it's great for my fitness, and I sleep much better afterwards. Moreover, being out in fresh air and away from screens gives me a mental break, and problems seem smaller up there. The group also matters, because I've made real friends, and we chat for hours on the trail.`,
   `Of course, it isn't always perfect. Sometimes it rains, and once we got lost for an hour. But even those moments become good stories later. I'd like to try a multi-day trek in the future.`]);
add('activity', 3, 'Describe something you do to stay healthy', ['what you do', 'how often you do it', 'when you started doing it'], 'how it helps you', 'daily walk, 30 minutes, since illness, sleep better, energy, mood', null);
add('activity', 2, 'Describe a game you enjoyed playing as a child', ['what the game was', 'who you played it with', 'where you played it'], 'why you enjoyed it', 'hide and seek, cousins, courtyard, evenings, laughter, imagination', null);
add('activity', 3, 'Describe a skill you would like to learn in the future', ['what the skill is', 'why you want to learn it', 'how you would learn it'], 'how it would help you',
  'play piano, always wanted, app lessons, teacher, 20 minutes daily, relax and perform',
  [`The skill I'd really like to learn is playing the piano. It's something that I've wanted to do since I was a child, but I never had the opportunity.`,
   `When I was about ten, a neighbour had an old piano in her living room, and I used to listen through the open window when her daughter practised. I remember thinking that the music sounded magical. My parents couldn't afford lessons or an instrument, so the idea faded, but it has come back every few years.`,
   `At the moment, I'm planning to start in the new year. I've already looked at a few options, and I think I'd begin with an electronic keyboard, because it's much cheaper than a real piano and I can use headphones, so I wouldn't disturb my neighbours. For lessons, I'd like to find a teacher who offers weekly sessions, maybe online, and I'd supplement them with a phone app that has short exercises. I'd aim to practise for twenty minutes a day, because regular practice is more effective than a long session once a week.`,
   `I believe that it would help me in several ways. For one thing, it would be a creative outlet that's completely different from my work. Playing music is also supposed to be good for concentration and memory. And I'd simply enjoy being able to play a song for my family at gatherings.`,
   `I realise that it will take years to get good, and I'll probably be frustrated at the start. But I'd rather begin now than wake up at sixty and regret that I never tried.`]);
add('activity', 4, 'Describe a volunteer activity or community project you know about', ['what the activity is', 'who organises it', 'what the volunteers do'], 'why you think it is worthwhile', 'community garden, neighbours, empty lot, vegetables, shared, school visits', null);

